      text: `It is a hot day in summer. A fox walks in the forest. He is very hungry and very thirsty.

The fox comes to a big garden. In the garden there is a tall tree. On the tree there are grapes. The grapes are big, round and purple. They look sweet and juicy.

"I want those grapes," says the fox. "They are perfect for a hot day."

The fox jumps. He cannot reach the grapes. He jumps again, higher this time. He still cannot reach them. The grapes are too high.

The fox goes back a few steps. He runs fast and jumps as high as he can. His nose almost touches the grapes, but he falls down on the grass.

He tries again and again. Now he is tired. His legs hurt, and he is still hungry.

At last the fox stops. He looks up at the grapes and lifts his nose in the air.

"I do not really want those grapes," he says. "I am sure they are sour. Only a silly animal eats sour grapes."

And the fox walks away, slowly and proudly.

Sometimes people are like the fox. When they cannot get something, they say that they never wanted it.`,
