        title: 'Charging and the Future',
        text: `Of course, the battery needs to be charged. Many owners simply plug the car in at home overnight, just like a phone. In the morning the battery is full again. On long journeys drivers use fast chargers along the motorway. A fast charger can fill most of the battery in twenty or thirty minutes, enough time for a coffee and a short break.

The distance a car can travel on one charge is called its range. Early electric cars had a range of about one hundred kilometres. Many modern models can drive four or five hundred kilometres before they need to stop. Cold weather reduces the range, because the battery works less well and the heating uses energy too.

Electric cars produce no exhaust gases, so the air in cities becomes cleaner. However, they are not completely "green". Making the batteries needs a lot of energy and rare metals, and the electricity itself may come from coal or gas power stations. Their real benefit depends on how clean the electricity is.

Engineers are working hard on better batteries: lighter, cheaper, faster to charge and easier to recycle. Many countries plan to stop selling new petrol cars in the next ten or twenty years. It seems likely that the quiet hum of an electric motor will soon be the normal sound of traffic.`,
