      text: `My name is Emma, and I am seventeen years old. Last summer I got my first job. I worked in a small café near the beach in my town.

Before the first day I was very nervous. I did not know how to make coffee, and I was afraid of making mistakes. The owner of the café, Mr Lewis, was a friendly man with a big moustache. "Don't worry," he said. "Everybody learns. Just smile and be polite."

My working day started at eight o'clock. First I cleaned the tables and put out the chairs. Then I helped in the kitchen. I cut fruit for the smoothies and made sandwiches.

At ten o'clock the customers started to come. Some wanted breakfast, some wanted only a cup of tea. I wrote their orders in a small notebook and carried the plates to the tables.

On my second day I dropped a whole tray of glasses. Everybody in the café looked at me. My face was red, and I wanted to disappear. But an old lady at the next table said, "Never mind, dear. That happens to everyone." Then she gave me a big tip.

After a few weeks I could make coffee, carry four plates at once and remember the names of the regular customers. I was tired in the evenings, but I was also proud.

With my money I bought a new bicycle. But the most important thing was not the money. I learned to talk to strangers, to work in a team and to stay calm when things go wrong.`,
